From 4a9e4a68d04163fe1f2995db73dcdba64f0ae23c
Subject: Web: run AsyncComputeTaskPool tasks on async compute workers
Origin: https://github.com/robtfm/bevy/commit/4a9e4a68d04163fe1f2995db73dcdba64f0ae23c
Date: 2026-10-16T14:05:08+01:00

On the web `TaskPool::spawn` runs its future on the calling thread's JS
event loop (the single-threaded web pool's behaviour, kept because asset
loading, fetches and other page-bound futures are not `Send` there), so a
`spawn` on the AsyncComputeTaskPool ran on the engine worker between
frames: a burst of 256 glyph rasters in the explorer stalled it for
150-250 ms. Only `scope` reached the pool's executor and workers, and
only the ComputeTaskPool had workers.

`web_worker::start` now also spawns `async_compute_threads` workers that
join the AsyncComputeTaskPool through `run_worker`, like the compute
workers do, and `AsyncComputeTaskPool` gets its own `spawn` on the web
that requires `Send` and hands the future to the pool's executor, so
those workers run it; until a worker has joined it falls back to the JS
event loop. `TaskPool::spawn` is unchanged for the other pools. Off the
web `spawn` already requires `Send`, so only wasm-specific futures (page
objects, wgpu objects) can fail the new bound, and they fail at compile
time: the pipeline cache's wasm branch spawns its (non-Send, synchronous
on wgpu/web) creation task with `spawn_local`, which is what web `spawn`
did, and the screenshot readback moves to the IoTaskPool, which only
waits on a buffer map.
